    "early_native_sum_note": "GRPO first-rollout contribution is the sum of its four per-update token suppression fractions divided by the sum over120 updates. This is86.51% of that unweighted sum, not the fraction of pooled suppressed tokens."
  },
  "interpretations": {
    "pretrain": "Loss drops steeply early and then more slowly, while the logged cosine learning rate decays. This demonstrates fitting the training objective, not heldout quality. Three superseded observations remain in the image.",
    "full_sft": "The mean loss is lower late in training but individual microbatches remain noisy. The last loss is above the final-window mean, so a single endpoint is a poor summary.",
    "dpo": "The final ten-observation mean is lower, but the final median is slightly higher and the curve is not monotonic. Sparse changing preference microbatches do not establish a smooth convergence trajectory. The apparent learning-rate staircase and zeros come from eight-decimal logging.",
    "cispo": "The late reward window is higher and the surrogate-loss mean lower, but reward retains substantial positive and negative variation. This is progress on the configured training reward, not proof of task correctness.",
    "grpo": "Reward shifts upward while the recorded objective rises from near zero and settles at a small positive scale. At K=1 the mean-zero group advantages can cancel much of the scalar surrogate value when ratios are near1, while the KL term remains and the gradient need not vanish. The rising scalar alone is not evidence of degradation or divergence.",
    "k4": "Repeated optimization of frozen rollouts moves importance ratios from near1; later fresh rollouts reset old-policy comparisons. The first rollout dominates observed extreme ratios and much of GRPO suppression. CISPO's cap is never reached in these samples. The measured average KL is similar, while individual peaks and endpoints differ; neither implies quality superiority."
  },
  "suggested_next_experiments": "None required for this figure readout. Testing generalization or an algorithm advantage would require separately scoped fixed-heldout evaluation and independent training seeds; no such result is inferred here."
